    "content": "Driving Under the Influence (DUI) in Virginia involves operating a vehicle with a blood alcohol content (BAC) of 0.08% or higher or under the influence of drugs. First-time offenses are Class 1 misdemeanors, punishable by up to 12 months in jail, up to $2,500 in fines, and a 12-month license suspension. Penalties for Driving Under the Influence First Offense: Up to 12 months in jail, $250–$2,500 in fines, and 12-month license suspension. Elevated BAC: A BAC of 0.15%–0.20% brings mandatory minimum jail time (5--10 days). Subsequent Offenses: Second offenses within 5 years require 20 days mandatory jail time; third offenses are Class 6 felonies. Other Penalties: Mandatory Alcohol Safety Action Program (ASAP) enrollment and ignition interlock device installation. Long-Term ConsequencesA DUI conviction creates a permanent criminal record, which cannot be expunged in Virginia. Long-term impacts include significantly higher auto insurance premiums, loss of employment or educational opportunities, potential loss of security clearances, and immigration issues for non-citizens. DUI Laws Regarding Drugs (DUID)Driving under the influence of drugs (including prescription medication) is illegal, with the same penalties as alcohol-related DUI. A DUID conviction also adds 6 demerit points to the driving record. Signs of Driving Under the InfluenceLaw enforcement identifies impaired drivers through erratic behavior, such as weaving between lanes, driving too slowly, improper passing, and delayed reaction times, such as stopping at green lights. Laws Regarding MinorsDrivers under 21 with a BAC of 0.02% to less than 0.08% can be convicted of illegal consumption of alcohol and face penalties.",
